    "created_at": "2026-04-14T14:45:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-04-14T14:45:09Z",
    "body": "> While at it, define how datalist rendering should behave if there is both label/innertext and value. Chrome/Safari's approach in displaying both is very nice from a UX standpoint, allows label/innertext to contain more description of the given choice.\n\nI'd argue the opposite.  A big downside to the approach is that often the underlying \"value\" isn't one that should be user visible and it's preferable to display only a formatted version.\n\nAs an example, consider phone numbers -- if you have the following defined as an option, you get completely different rendering behavior in a <datalist> vs. in a <select>:\n```\n<option value=\"8005551212\">(800) 555-1212</option>\n```\n\nYou could achieve the same thing that they're doing with much more flexibility by allowing for html elements as children of <option> in all contexts that it's used, like:\n```\n<option value=\"chocolate\">\n<b>Chocolate</b>\n<br/>\nDark Chocolate, nutty flavor\n</option>\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
